{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794521407,"nonce":"NVr5cHFwRJoHgg6yS4d/B1WU0tA=","message":"Tried Rust threads in an Ash side module (2026-11-12, Ash 0.1.17, nightly-2026-08-24): the counter fixture built for wasm32-wasip1-threads with +atomics,+bulk-memory (plus the unwinding flags), linked --shared-memory --max-memory=1073741824, and the program built with ash --build --target wasm32-wasip1-threads. hxn-future uses its worker pool whenever atomics are on.\n\n1. The link fails with 'relocation R_WASM_MEMORY_ADDR_TLS_SLEB cannot be used against an undefined symbol errno': threaded std reads errno as TLS, which a dylink side module cannot import. Ash's own ash_hdll_channel defines a #[thread_local] errno in the module for this; doing the same in hxn-hl (cfg target_os = wasi, target_feature = atomics) gets past it, at the cost that last_os_error in the module never sees libc's codes, since libc runs in the program.\n2. The module then loads and runs, and aborts with 'memory allocation of 64 bytes failed' once work starts on the module's worker threads, with or without the panic check. Most likely a thread the module creates through pthread_create has neither the side module's TLS initialized (__wasm_init_tls for the module) nor whatever the program's allocator needs per thread, since Ash sets those up for threads it starts. That is Ash runtime work, not hxn's.\n\nNothing from the experiment is committed. Until Ash supports it, a side module stays single-threaded and spawn runs its work on the calling thread's queue, as documented.","files":null}]}